 # Epipolar Sampling
 
+Also called: Epipolar Line Sampling, Cross-View Epipolar Sampling
 
 ## Overview
-Epipolar sampling is a technique to sample features from other views along epipolar lines for a given ray, enabling cross-view feature aggregation without full 3D volume rendering during the encoding phase [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently correlating information between a reference view and target views without performing full 3D volume rendering or explicit 3D reconstruction during the encoding phase [S1, unofficial].
+Epipolar sampling is a technique for aggregating features from multiple views by sampling along epipolar lines rather than along the camera ray in 3D space. It solves the problem of scale ambiguity in 3D reconstruction from sparse views, where camera poses are scaled by an arbitrary factor, preventing the inference of metric depth from a single view [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard volume rendering which samples along a ray, this method samples features from a reference pixel and correlates them with features sampled along the corresponding epipolar line in a second view, augmented with positionally encoded depths [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- `features`: Tensor of image features for all views.
-- `intrinsics`: Camera intrinsic matrices.
-- `extrinsics`: Camera-to-world transformation matrices (OpenCV-style, +Z forward) [S1, unofficial].
-- `near`, `far`: Near and far plane distances for ray projection.
-- `num_samples` ($s$): Number of samples per epipolar line segment. Typical value: 64 [S1, unofficial].
+- Source view features and camera parameters (intrinsics $K$, extrinsics $W_{cw}$).
